From: Roman Senn <roman.l.senn@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ws: permessage-deflate: do not drop the first inflated chunk after a bulk payload

A server receiving a permessage-deflate message that inflates to more than a
few rx buffers loses part of it: no LWS_CALLBACK_RECEIVE is made for the
first chunk of output that the inflater yields after the payload was
consumed in bulk, so the application gets the message with holes in it, and
the message is shorter than the one that was sent.  Nothing is reported,
there is no error, the final fragment arrives as usual.

With the default rx buffer (the inflater yields up to rx_buffer_size bytes
at a time, 1024 here) a message of 70000 bytes arrived as 52592 bytes, of
1000000 as 747205: about a quarter of the data is missing.  Messages
below about 4000 bytes are not affected, which is why this goes unnoticed
with small messages.

Cause
-----

lws_parse_ws() takes a data frame's payload in bulk when it is in the
buffer it was given (lws_ws_frame_rest_is_payload()): that hands the bytes
to the extension and, when it is done, calls

    lws_ws_rx_sm(wsi, ALREADY_PROCESSED_IGNORE_CHAR | ALREADY_PROCESSED_NO_CB, 0)

to "deal with the ramifications".  NO_CB is there because the bulk path has
already made the user callback for what it processed itself.

When that is a payload that the inflater cannot finish in one go
(PMDR_HAS_PENDING), the wsi is on the pt's rx_draining_ext list, and
lws_ws_rx_sm() entered in LWS_RXPS_NEW with wsi->ws->rx_draining_ext set
takes the wsi off the list at the top (lws_remove_wsi_from_draining_ext_list(),
which also clears wsi->ws->rx_draining_ext) and notes that in its local
rx_draining_ext before it runs the extension for one more chunk of output.

The user callback for that chunk is then skipped, because the place that
clears NO_CB for a drain tests the wsi flag, which the entry has just
cleared:

    if (wsi->ws->rx_draining_ext)
        already_processed &= ~ALREADY_PROCESSED_NO_CB;

The extension adds the wsi to the list again only if it has still more to
deliver after this chunk, i.e. the flag is only set again for the later
chunks.  So the chunk produced by the very call that came out of the bulk
path is discarded, and the following ones are delivered, until the
next bulk pass.  Draining that does not follow a bulk pass is
unaffected (NO_CB is not set).

The fix
-------

Also test the local rx_draining_ext, which records that this call entered as
a drain, so NO_CB is cleared for it as well.  The user callback is then
made for that chunk as it is for every other.  NO_CB is
cleared only for a call that is a drain: the bulk path's own callback was
for the bytes it processed itself, and the output of a drain is not
among them.

Reproducer
----------

A standalone C program, no application code involved: an lws server with
permessage-deflate and a child process that does the handshake over a plain
socket and sends one compressed binary message of N bytes in one write(),
so that the whole frame is in the server's first read and its payload
is taken in bulk.  (With an lws client as the sender I could not trigger
it, so this is how the frame is delivered by the peers that did: python
websockets.)  The server
counts and verifies the bytes it gets in LWS_CALLBACK_RECEIVE and exits
non-zero if they are not all the same ones that were sent.  It needs only
zlib.  Attached as ws-deflate-large-message.c; build with

    cc -o repro ws-deflate-large-message.c -I<lws>/include -I<build> \
       <build>/lib/libwebsockets.a -lz -lcap -lpthread

    $ ./repro 70000                   # before
    sent 70000 bytes, received 52592, 51353 wrong       (exit 1)
    $ ./repro 70000                   # after
    sent 70000 bytes, received 70000, 0 wrong           (exit 0)

Sizes below 4000 bytes pass before and after, 4095 gives 3071 before,
65536 gives 49152, 131072 gives 98304, 1000000 gives 747205; all sizes
tried (1 to 1000000) are complete after.

Found with a QuickJS server (qjs-lws) whose websocket messages were cut
short by a python websockets client sending 70000 bytes.

Tested on x86_64 Linux, poll event loop, no TLS, against current main.
Made against libwebsockets f1650e3e7 (main).

Signed-off-by: Roman Senn <roman.l.senn@gmail.com>

---
diff --git a/lib/sansio/ws/ops-ws.c b/lib/sansio/ws/ops-ws.c
index 9a570c5dd..05a5c0a38 100644
--- a/lib/sansio/ws/ops-ws.c
+++ b/lib/sansio/ws/ops-ws.c
@@ -875,7 +875,14 @@ drain_extension:
 			n = lws_ext_cb_active(wsi, LWS_EXT_CB_PAYLOAD_RX, &pmdrx, 0);
 			lwsl_debug("%s: ext says %d / ebuf.len %d\n", __func__,
 				   n, pmdrx.eb_out.len);
-			if (wsi->ws->rx_draining_ext)
+			/*
+			 * The entry drain took us off the list above, so the
+			 * wsi flag alone is clear again for a drain that came
+			 * in with nothing left pending after it: use what we
+			 * entered with too, or a drain that the bulk path
+			 * called with NO_CB has its output swallowed
+			 */
+			if (wsi->ws->rx_draining_ext || rx_draining_ext)
 				already_processed &= (char)~ALREADY_PROCESSED_NO_CB;
 #endif
 
